      "d": "Sound rules",
      "t": "rule",
      "f": "How is gn pronounced?",
      "m": "Like ny in canyon. One sound, not two. gnocchi, signore, bagno, lasagne."
    },
    {
      "d": "Sound rules",
      "t": "rule",
      "f": "How is gli pronounced?",
      "m": "Like lli in million, but softer and further back. famiglia, figlio, meglio, aglio."
    },
    {
      "d": "Sound rules",
      "t": "rule",
      "f": "How is sc pronounced?",
      "m": "Sh before e and i — pesce, sciare. Sk before a, o, u — scuola, scarpa. Same rule as c and g."
    },
    {
      "d": "Sound rules",
      "t": "rule",
      "f": "What does a double consonant do in Italian?",
      "m": "It is genuinely held longer, and it changes meaning: nonno and nono are different words."
    },
